    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 081803\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1103 AM PDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Well above-normal temperatures will persist through Friday, with \n  dangerous heat expected in Death Valley.\n\n* Chances for gusty winds, cooler temperatures, and potentially\n  widespread precipitation arrive Sunday through Tuesday. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through the middle part of next week.\n\nHigh pressure persists through Friday, maintaining hot and dry \nconditions across the forecast area. Afternoon high temperatures \nwill be 10 to 15 degrees above normal for early October, including \nhighs in the mid-to-upper 90s for Las Vegas. Highs in the upper 100s \nwill result in Major Heat Risk in Death Valley, where an Extreme \nHeat Warning remains in effect through Friday. Multiple daily high \ntemperature and warm minimum temperature records are in jeopardysee \nthe CLIMATE section below for details.\n\nAttention turns to a major pattern shift this weekend involving an \nupper-level trough, a weak low, and Hurricane Rachel. Rachel is \ncurrently situated around 123W 22N and slowly drifting west-\nnorthwest at 5 to 10 mph. Rachel is expected to continue this WNW \ntrack over the next couple of days, but will then interact with a \ncutoff low dropping south off southern California. Once this occurs, \nRachel is expected to make a sharp turn to the northeast, eventually \ngetting pulled into northern Baja and then moving into southern \nCalifornia, western Arizona, and southern Nevada by Sunday. This \nwill also interact with a deepening trough over the West Coast that \nis forecast to close off over central California and eventually \ntrack east over southern Nevada. Any change in the track of Rachel, \nthe depth of the West Coast trough, or the placement of the southern \nCalifornia low could alter the forecasted location and amount of \nprecipitation late this weekend and early next week. However, the \nlatest runs indicate that areas of southern Nevada, northwest \nArizona, and eastern San Bernardino County could potentially see \nbetween 0.50\" and 1.00\" of rain by early next week, with higher \namounts over terrain. Moisture will likely stick around at least \nthrough mid-week, bringing shower chances Monday through Wednesday \nas the low passes through the region. Colder air associated with the \nlow could also bring some snow to the higher peaks of the Spring \nMountains, though snow levels will generally remain above 9,000 \nfeet. Expect gusty winds Saturday, with most areas seeing gusts \nbetween 25 and 35 mph, increasing to 30 to 40 mph by Sunday. \nTemperatures will cool significantly this weekend, dropping around \n10 to 15 degrees from Friday's highs, with even cooler temperatures \nexpected early to mid-next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Light \nwinds under 8KT following typical daytime and nighttime directions \nare expected today and tonight. After a period of light east winds \nFriday morning, southeast winds will increase around 21Z Friday with \ngusts 15-20KT possible through the afternoon. VFR skies with \nafternoon FEW-SCT clouds around 12kft.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds will follow \ntypical daily directional patterns with speeds of 10KT or less for \nmost locations. The exception will be in the lower Colorado River \nValley, mainly KIFP, where breezy northerly winds with gusts to \naround 20KT developing this afternoon. VFR conditions are expected \nareawide with cloud bases at or above 10kft. \n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE....Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           THU, OCT 8  FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     98(2024)*   97(2024)*\nBishop        95(1996)    93(2015)\nNeedles       107(2024)   104(1996)*\nDaggett       101(1996)*  103(1980)\nKingman       98(1996)    98(1910)\nDesert Rock   98(1996)*   97(1996)\nDeath Valley  112(1996)*  112(1996)\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      THU, OCT 8  FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     76(2024)*   72(2024)*\nBishop        51(1960)*   61(2025)\nNeedles       76(2024)*   76(1988)*\nDaggett       69(1980)*   69(1996)*\nKingman       67(1960)*   68(1926)*\nDesert Rock   70(2024)    65(1991)*\nDeath Valley  85(1964)*   78(2024)*\n\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Gorelow\nAVIATION...Nickerson\n\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
